Chapter: BuiltinNameShadow
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 A user definition may carry the same name as a builtin, and `emit-name`
 checks `is-builtin` BEFORE it checks the user-arity table, so the builtin
 wins the lookup and the user's own definition is reached only by the call
 that the fallback emits. `codex/foreword/ui/Vector.codex:192` is a live
 instance: `vec-sub : Integer = 64`, shadowing the SIMD `vec-sub`.

 THIS IS A COMPILE-TIME ARM. It went in after main 19551 shipped a seed that
 crashed the compiler outright on exactly this shape: the nullary-builtin
 route handed the shadowed name's emitter an EMPTY argument list, and
 `emit-vec-arith-core` indexed argument 0 of it (`!EXC=06`, RSI=0). Anything
 citing `GopIcon` went down with it, which is most of the desk. The rows below
 are a bonus; the file COMPILING is the measurement.

 A shadowing name must be nullary to be worth arming, because a nullary use
 site is the one that reaches the bare-name path at all. `vec-mul` is here so
 the arm does not rest on a single builtin's emitter.

Section: Subjects

  vec-sub : Integer = 64

  vec-mul : Integer = 3

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
   print-line-uni ("shadowed constant want 64: " & show vec-sub)
   print-line-uni ("shadowed product  want 192: " & show (vec-sub * vec-mul))
  end
